<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Chalco</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Chalco was a freshwater lake in the south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>, joined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-xochimilco">Lake Xochimilco</a> and part of the lake system that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>. Its shores were home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalca">Chalca</a> city-states, and chinampa fields in its shallows helped feed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. It was drained in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, and much of its former bed is now built over.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-chalco.jpg" alt="Lake Chalco" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlfaBeta0104 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuevo_Lago_de_Chalco.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
